  "notes": "Both files, SCBL_Loan_Restructured.xlsx (1st Restructured) and \"SCBL_Loan_2nd Restructured.xlsx\" (2nd Restructured), each contain a FULL from-origination (2019-09-15) schedule rather than a schedule starting only from their respective effective dates — they are complete model rebuilds superseding the prior version in full, exactly like the original SCBL_Loan.xlsx workbook (not read here per task scope, but referenced for divergence-point cross-checking). I row-by-row diffed each successive pair of full schedules (original vs 1st Restructured; 1st vs 2nd Restructured) to find the exact installment where they first disagree — this pinpoints the true effective date and rolled-forward opening balance for each version, all of which is captured above as override arrays (opening/interest/closing balance amounts) because the underlying rate is a floating/declining quarterly rate (visible in the \"SCBL_Loan\" sheet's \"Quarterly Interest Rate\" row, apparently tied to a refinancing benchmark) that cannot be cleanly reproduced from a single first-principles fixed rate.\n\nEvent 1 (1st Restructure, file \"SCBL_Loan_Restructured.xlsx\"): schedules match the original exactly through the 2021-03-15 installment (opening 1,700,000,000, interest 38,250,000 in both), then diverge starting the 2021-06-15 installment — the restructured version keeps opening balance at 1,700,000,000 (still interest-only) where the original had already begun amortizing (would have closed at 1,649,680,000). This reflects the \"Moratorium period (Principal)\" extension documented in the SCBL_Loan sheet: 18 months (ending 2021-01-02) → 24 months (ending 2021-07-02), and \"Number of installments\" (principal-bearing) 34→32. Overall tenor and maturity date (2029-06-14) were NOT changed — only the moratorium/grace period. Rate-wise, the effective quarterly-annualized rate also dropped modestly across this event (9.0% → 8.51% at the divergence point) as part of the floating-rate mechanism, so this is coded as RESTRUCTURE (moratorium extension is the dominant driver, not a pure rate change) rather than MORATORIUM_EXTENSION alone, since a rate/tenor-relevant term also shifted. capitalizedAmount/overdueAmountRolledIn = 0 — no unpaid interest or overdue amounts were folded into principal; the deferred interest during the extra grace quarters continued to be paid in full each quarter per the accrual rows (not capitalized).\n\nEvent 2 (2nd Restructure, file \"SCBL_Loan_2nd Restructured.xlsx\"): matches the 1st Restructured schedule exactly through 2021-06-15 (opening 1,700,000,000, interest 36,975,080.75 in both), then diverges starting 2021-09-15 — same opening balance (1,700,000,000, i.e., no capitalization/overdue roll-in) but a materially lower effective rate (8.12%→7.42% at the divergence point, continuing to trend down toward ~6.0-6.6% in later periods per the \"Quarterly Interest Rate\" / \"Expected Interest Rate\" rows) and principal re-amortized over 34 installments of a flat 50,000,000/quarter (vs. 32 installments of 53,125,000/quarter), which extends final maturity from 2029-06-14 to 2029-12-15 (+2 quarters / 6 months). Classified as RESTRUCTURE (both rate track and tenor/installment count changed).\n\nNo cell text, comments, or document properties in either workbook cite a specific regulatory circular, BB approval reference, or COVID-19 justification for either restructure — regulatoryReference is set to null for both events. The timing (mid-2021, moratorium/grace extensions) is consistent with the general pattern of COVID-19-era NBFI loan relief in Bangladesh, but I did not find an explicit citation in-file to confirm this, so I have not asserted one. Workbook lastModifiedBy metadata: 1st Restructured file last modified 2021-06-28 (Rezwana Ali Tanima); 2nd Restructured file last modified 2025-12-15 (same author) — the latter is very likely just a re-open/recalculation timestamp, not the actual 2nd-restructure execution date, since the embedded schedule data itself diverges starting the 2021-09-15 installment (consistent with a restructure negotiated/approved shortly after the 1st one, in Q3 2021), not 2025.\n\nBoth workbooks model the same three underlying disbursement tranches (499,981,000 + 200,019,000 on 2019-07-02, and 1,000,000,000 on 2019-07-16, totaling 1,700,000,000) broken out on separate internal sub-schedule blocks in the \"SCBL_Loan\" sheet that are then summed into the single \"Repayment Schedule\" sheet used for all figures above; only the combined/aggregate figures were used to build the params/verification objects per the task's single-disbursement roll-forward instruction. num_installments in each version's params reflects only the FUTURE installment count from that version's effective date forward (33 for 1st Restructure, 34 for 2nd Restructure), consistent with modeling each version as a fresh single-disbursement loan starting on its effective date."
